How to make a good Prompt Engineering
	be specific
	use clear Instructions
	provide context
		If you're asking for something technical or complex, giving some background information can help the model provide a more tailored answer.
	ask follow up questions
		don’t hesitate to build on the initial prompt.
	Be Mindful of Length:
		Short prompts can be too broad, while excessively long prompts can sometimes confuse the model. Aim for a balance.
	Use Open-ended vs. Close-ended Prompts:
		open-ended prompts give more room for detailed, thoughtful responses, they start with "what how why".
		close-ended prompt give shorter, more specific answers and are good when you need clear facts.
	Use Constraints for Style/Tone:
		If you're looking for a certain tone or style, mention it in your prompt.
	Request for Sources or References (if applicable)
		If you need answers backed by data, ask for sources or explain where the model’s information should come from.
	Use Examples or Templates:
		If you want a response to follow a specific structure, providing an example can be helpful.
	Leverage “Roleplaying” for Creativity:
		Sometimes, asking the model to take on a specific role can make its answers more creative or targeted.






RAG(Retrieval-Augmented Generation)
	Understanding waht is RAG.
		In essence, RAG combines the power of information retrieval and text generation, which enables the AI system to generate responses based on both pre-trained knowledge and up-to-date information pulled from external sources.
		Retrieval Component
		Generate Componet
	Data Preparation & Knowledge Base:
		Curate a Knowledge Base: You’ll need a well-organized, structured knowledge base that the retrieval component can query. This could be in the form of documents, web pages, or even structured databases.
			Consider using technologies like Elasticsearch, FAISS (Facebook AI Similarity Search), or BM25 for efficient document retrieval.
			Make sure the knowledge base is up-to-date and includes domain-specific knowledge relevant to your AI agent’s use case.
		Document Embedding: The documents or knowledge you want to retrieve from should be converted into embeddings (vector representations) using models like Sentence-BERT, OpenAI embeddings, or T5. 
		This helps make the retrieval process more efficient and accurate.
	Retrieval Model (Information Retrieval Layer):
		Use an Efficient Search Algorithm:You’ll want to use algorithms that can quickly search through large amounts of data and return the most relevant documents.
			Vector-based Search: Use pre-trained embedding models to transform your documents into vectors. Then, use a search engine like FAISS to retrieve the closest vectors to the query vector.
			BM25: A traditional but effective retrieval algorithm based on term frequency and inverse document frequency (TF-IDF), which can be used for document ranking.
	Design the Generation Model (Transformer-based):
		you’ll need a text generation model that can effectively use this information to generate the desired output.
			T5 (Text-to-Text Transfer Transformer) or GPT-3: Fine-tuning these models on your specific domain will allow the agent to generate high-quality responses.
			BART or T5 for Summarization: If your system retrieves lengthy documents, models like BART (Bidirectional and Auto-Regressive Transformers) or T5 can be used to generate summaries or condensed responses.
	Fine-tuning & Integration:
		End-to-End Model Training: To integrate the retrieval and generation components, you’ll need to fine-tune them together so the generation model knows how to utilize the information retrieved by the search model.
			Retrieval-Augmented Training: Train the retrieval model and the generator together. You can use a hybrid setup where, during training, the generator is given access to both the query and relevant documents to learn how to combine them effectively.
		Reinforcement Learning (RLHF - Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback): You might also want to incorporate human feedback loops to fine-tune the RAG system further, especially when the system is tasked with handling complex or nuanced queries.				





AI Agent
What is AI Agent?  In simple terms, AI Agent is something that accomplish tasks using AI. 
	It refers to a system or component that performs actions autonomously in an environment based on certain inputs or stimuli. 
	It could be a part of a complex AI System and 
	It is used to interacted with their enviroment, leran from it, and make decisions to achieve a specific goal.

Types of AI Agents
	Reactive Agents: they response to users immediately and they don`t "remmember" previous states. 
		For example, they response to users based on input without long-term-memory.
	Deliberative Agents: they have a model of the world, make decisions by reasoning about their environment. they may consider the past states and plan for the future.
		For example, Self-driving cars make dicisions based on traffic data and inputs of sensors 
	Learning Agents: they improve performances over time by learning from expierences and use technique like reinforcement to adjust their behavior based on feedback.
		For example, A robot that learns the best way to navigate an unknown environment by trial and error.
	Autonomous Agents: These Agents operate independently, using internal decision-making processes to perform tasks wihtout contastant human oversight. They may combine
	various AI techniques(Machine learning, planning, perception) to navigate dynamic environments. 	
	
	


How Agents Work
	Perception： The agent collects data from its environment, which can be in the form of sensors, input signals, or through interaction with external systems (e.g., web scraping, reading a database).
	Reasoning/Decision-Making: After perceiving the environment, the agent processes the data and makes decisions based on predefined rules, algorithms, or learned patterns. 
		This could involve things like:
			Logic-based reasoning (if-else rules)
			Machine learning models for pattern recognition
			Planning algorithms for future actions
	Action： Once the decision is made, the agent acts by performing some action, which could be interacting with the environment, triggering external processes, or updating its internal state. For example, an agent in a coding environment might optimize a piece of code based on the feedback it gets.
	Learning： Some agents improve their performance over time using techniques like:
				Reinforcement learning
				Supervised learning
				Unsupervised learning





How to Use Agents Effectively
	Clear Goal Definition： Agents need clear, well-defined goals.
	Choosing the Right Algorithm：
		If the task is deterministic and rule-based, a reactive agent might work best.
		If you need to plan actions over time, a deliberative agent may be better.
		For tasks involving exploration, optimization, or improving over time, a learning agent would be more suitable.
	Provide Sufficient Feedback:
		If the agent uses machine learning or reinforcement learning, providing accurate and consistent feedback will help the agent improve over time. This feedback loop is crucial for refining the agent’s performance.
	Environment Modeling: 
		If you're working with agents that learn or plan based on their environment (e.g., a game environment or business process), accurately modeling the environment is key. 
		This includes understanding how the agent will interact with various states and conditions.
	Monitoring and Adjusting: Even if an agent is autonomous, you should regularly monitor its performance. Over time, agents may require adjustments or re-training based on shifts in data, requirements, or environments.
	Resource Management: Agents, especially in complex environments, may need to be efficient in resource utilization. If your agent is resource-hungry, it can slow down processes. Optimize for computational efficiency, memory use, and overall performance.





